  2→title: Persistence
  3→description: Learn how to save and load Automerge documents, use incremental updates, and work with change history
  4→---
  5→
  6→# Overview
  7→
  8→Automerge documents can be saved to binary format and loaded later.
  9→
 10→This enables persistent storage in databases, files, or any binary storage system. The library supports both full saves and incremental updates for efficiency.
 11→
 12→# Saving Documents
 13→
 14→Save a complete document to bytes using `Save`:
 15→
 16→```csharp
 17→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 18→var root = doc.RootId;
 19→
 20→doc.PutString(root, "title", "My Document");
 21→doc.PutString(root, "content", "Document content here");
 22→doc.Commit("Initial version");
 23→
 24→// Save to bytes
 25→var bytes = doc.Save();
 26→Console.WriteLine($"Document size: {bytes.Length} bytes");
 27→
 28→// Save to file
 29→File.WriteAllBytes("document.automerge", bytes);
 30→```
 31→
 32→The `Save` method returns a byte array containing the complete document with all history.
 33→
 34→# Loading Documents
 35→
 36→Load a saved document using `Load`:
 37→
 38→```csharp
 39→// Load from file
 40→var bytes = File.ReadAllBytes("document.automerge");
 41→
 42→// Restore the document
 43→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Load(bytes);
 44→
 45→// Verify the content
 46→var json = doc.HydrateJson();
 47→Console.WriteLine(json);
 48→// Shows: {"title": "My Document", "content": "Document content here"}
 49→```
 50→
 51→The loaded document contains all data and change history from the saved state.
 52→
 53→# Error Handling
 54→
 55→Handle load failures gracefully:
 56→
 57→```csharp
 58→try
 59→{
 60→    var bytes = File.ReadAllBytes("document.automerge");
 61→    using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Load(bytes);
 62→    Console.WriteLine("Document loaded successfully");
 63→}
 64→catch (AutomergeException ex)
 65→{
 66→    Console.WriteLine($"Failed to load document: {ex.Message}");
 67→    // Handle corrupted or invalid data
 68→}
 69→catch (FileNotFoundException)
 70→{
 71→    Console.WriteLine("Document file not found");
 72→    // Create a new document instead
 73→    using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 74→}
 75→```
 76→
 77→Invalid or corrupted data throws an `AutomergeException`.
 78→
 79→# Incremental Saves
 80→
 81→For large documents that change frequently, use incremental saves:
 82→
 83→```csharp
 84→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
 85→var root = doc.RootId;
 86→
 87→// Initial state
 88→doc.PutString(root, "title", "Document");
 89→doc.Commit("Initial");
 90→
 91→// Save full document
 92→var fullSave = doc.Save();
 93→Console.WriteLine($"Full save: {fullSave.Length} bytes");
 94→
 95→// Make changes
 96→doc.PutString(root, "author", "Alice");
 97→doc.PutInt64(root, "version", 2);
 98→doc.Commit("Update metadata");
 99→
100→// Save only the changes since last save
101→var incrementalSave = doc.SaveIncremental();
102→Console.WriteLine($"Incremental save: {incrementalSave.Length} bytes");
103→
104→// Incremental is much smaller
105→var reduction = (1 - (double)incrementalSave.Length / fullSave.Length) * 100;
106→Console.WriteLine($"Size reduction: {reduction:F1}%");
107→```
108→
109→Incremental saves contain only changes since the last save or load, significantly reducing storage and network costs.
110→
111→# Loading Incremental Updates
112→
113→Apply incremental updates to an existing document:
114→
115→```csharp
116→// Start with the base document
117→var baseBytes = File.ReadAllBytes("document.automerge");
118→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Load(baseBytes);
119→
120→// Load incremental updates
121→var incrementalBytes = File.ReadAllBytes("document-update.automerge");
122→var changeCount = doc.LoadIncremental(incrementalBytes);
123→
124→Console.WriteLine($"Applied {changeCount} change(s)");
125→
126→// Document now contains both base and incremental changes
127→```
128→
129→The `LoadIncremental` method returns the number of changes applied.
130→
131→# Working with Streams
132→
133→Load incremental updates from byte spans:
134→
135→```csharp
136→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
137→
138→// Load from ReadOnlySpan<byte>
139→ReadOnlySpan<byte> incrementalData = GetIncrementalDataFromNetwork();
140→var count = doc.LoadIncremental(incrementalData);
141→
142→Console.WriteLine($"Applied {count} change(s) from stream");
143→```
144→
145→This avoids copying data when working with spans.
146→
147→# Change History
148→
149→Documents maintain complete change history.
150→
151→## Getting Heads
152→
153→Retrieve the current heads (latest commits):
154→
155→```csharp
156→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
157→var root = doc.RootId;
158→
159→doc.PutString(root, "field", "value1");
160→doc.Commit("Change 1");
161→
162→doc.PutString(root, "field", "value2");
163→doc.Commit("Change 2");
164→
165→var heads = doc.GetHeads();
166→Console.WriteLine($"Current heads: {heads.Count}");
167→foreach (var head in heads)
168→{
169→    Console.WriteLine($"  {head}");
170→}
171→```
172→
173→Most documents have one head. Multiple heads appear temporarily during merges until they converge.
174→
175→## Getting Changes
176→
177→Retrieve all changes since a known state:
178→
179→```csharp
180→// Get all changes
181→var allChanges = doc.GetChanges(new string[0]);
182→Console.WriteLine($"Total changes: {allChanges.Count}");
183→
184→// Get changes since specific heads
185→var knownHeads = new[] { "abc123def456..." };
186→var newChanges = doc.GetChanges(knownHeads);
187→Console.WriteLine($"New changes: {newChanges.Count}");
188→```
189→
190→Each change is a binary object you can send to other peers or store separately.
191→
192→## Applying Changes
193→
194→Apply changes to another document:
195→
196→```csharp
197→// Get changes from one document
198→var changes = doc1.GetChanges(new string[0]);
199→
200→// Apply to another document
201→doc2.ApplyChanges(changes);
202→
203→// Documents now have the same history
204→```
205→
206→This provides an alternative to the sync protocol for one-time transfers.
207→
208→## Last Local Change
209→
210→Get the most recent change made by this actor:
211→
212→```csharp
213→var lastChange = doc.GetLastLocalChange();
214→
215→if (lastChange != null)
216→{
217→    Console.WriteLine($"Last change size: {lastChange.Length} bytes");
218→
219→    // Store or broadcast this change
220→    await BroadcastChange(lastChange);
221→}
222→```
223→
224→This helps implement change notification systems.
225→
226→# Complete Example
227→
228→Here's a document management system with persistence:
229→
230→```csharp
231→using System;
232→using System.IO;
233→using System.Text.Json;
234→using Ray.Toolkit.Automerge;
235→
236→class DocumentStore
237→{
238→    readonly string _basePath;
239→
240→    public DocumentStore(string basePath)
241→    {
242→        _basePath = basePath;
243→        Directory.CreateDirectory(basePath);
244→    }
245→
246→    public AutomergeDocument CreateDocument(string id)
247→    {
248→        var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
249→        var root = doc.RootId;
250→
251→        doc.PutString(root, "id", id);
252→        doc.PutTimestamp(root, "created_at", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToUnixTimeMilliseconds());
253→        doc.Commit("Create document");
254→
255→        SaveDocument(id, doc);
256→        return doc;
257→    }
258→
259→    public AutomergeDocument LoadDocument(string id)
260→    {
261→        var path = GetDocumentPath(id);
262→
263→        if (!File.Exists(path))
264→        {
265→            throw new FileNotFoundException($"Document not found: {id}");
266→        }
267→
268→        var bytes = File.ReadAllBytes(path);
269→        return AutomergeDocument.Load(bytes);
270→    }
271→
272→    public void SaveDocument(string id, AutomergeDocument doc)
273→    {
274→        var path = GetDocumentPath(id);
275→        var bytes = doc.Save();
276→        File.WriteAllBytes(path, bytes);
277→
278→        Console.WriteLine($"Saved document {id} ({bytes.Length} bytes)");
279→    }
280→
281→    public void SaveIncremental(string id, AutomergeDocument doc)
282→    {
283→        var incrementalPath = GetIncrementalPath(id);
284→        var bytes = doc.SaveIncremental();
285→
286→        if (bytes.Length > 0)
287→        {
288→            File.AppendAllBytes(incrementalPath, bytes);
289→            Console.WriteLine($"Saved incremental update for {id} ({bytes.Length} bytes)");
290→        }
291→    }
292→
293→    public void ApplyIncrementalUpdates(string id, AutomergeDocument doc)
294→    {
295→        var incrementalPath = GetIncrementalPath(id);
296→
297→        if (!File.Exists(incrementalPath))
298→        {
299→            return;
300→        }
301→
302→        var bytes = File.ReadAllBytes(incrementalPath);
303→        var changeCount = doc.LoadIncremental(bytes);
304→
305→        Console.WriteLine($"Applied {changeCount} incremental update(s)");
306→
307→        // After applying, save full document and clear incrementals
308→        SaveDocument(id, doc);
309→        File.Delete(incrementalPath);
310→    }
311→
312→    string GetDocumentPath(string id) => Path.Combine(_basePath, $"{id}.automerge");
313→    string GetIncrementalPath(string id) => Path.Combine(_basePath, $"{id}.incremental");
314→}
315→
316→// Usage
317→var store = new DocumentStore("./documents");
318→
319→// Create a new document
320→using (var doc = store.CreateDocument("doc-001"))
321→{
322→    var root = doc.RootId;
323→    doc.PutString(root, "title", "Project Plan");
324→    doc.PutString(root, "author", "Alice");
325→    doc.Commit("Initial content");
326→
327→    store.SaveDocument("doc-001", doc);
328→}
329→
330→// Load and modify
331→using (var doc = store.LoadDocument("doc-001"))
332→{
333→    Console.WriteLine("Current state:");
334→    Console.WriteLine(doc.HydrateJson());
335→
336→    var root = doc.RootId;
337→    doc.PutString(root, "status", "in-progress");
338→    doc.Commit("Update status");
339→
340→    // Save incremental changes
341→    store.SaveIncremental("doc-001", doc);
342→}
343→
344→// Load and apply all updates
345→using (var doc = store.LoadDocument("doc-001"))
346→{
347→    store.ApplyIncrementalUpdates("doc-001", doc);
348→
349→    Console.WriteLine("Updated state:");
350→    Console.WriteLine(doc.HydrateJson());
351→}
352→```
353→
354→# Storage Strategies
355→
356→## Full Saves
357→
358→Periodically save the complete document:
359→
360→**Pros:**
361→- Simple to implement
362→- One file per document
363→- Easy to backup
364→
365→**Cons:**
366→- Larger storage size
367→- Slower for big documents
368→- Wastes space if mostly unchanged
369→
370→**Use when:**
371→- Documents are small
372→- Changes are infrequent
373→- Simplicity matters more than efficiency
374→
375→## Incremental Saves
376→
377→Save changes incrementally and periodically compact:
378→
379→**Pros:**
380→- Efficient storage
381→- Fast saves
382→- Good for frequent changes
383→
384→**Cons:**
385→- More complex implementation
386→- Need compaction strategy
387→- Multiple files per document
388→
389→**Use when:**
390→- Documents are large
391→- Changes are frequent
392→- Storage efficiency matters
393→
394→## Hybrid Approach
395→
396→Combine both strategies:
397→
398→```csharp
399→public void SaveDocument(string id, AutomergeDocument doc, bool forceFullSave = false)
400→{
401→    var incrementalPath = GetIncrementalPath(id);
402→    var incrementalSize = File.Exists(incrementalPath) ? new FileInfo(incrementalPath).Length : 0;
403→
404→    // Force full save if incremental updates grow too large
405→    if (forceFullSave || incrementalSize > 1024 * 1024) // 1 MB threshold
406→    {
407→        var bytes = doc.Save();
408→        File.WriteAllBytes(GetDocumentPath(id), bytes);
409→
410→        // Clear incremental file
411→        if (File.Exists(incrementalPath))
412→        {
413→            File.Delete(incrementalPath);
414→        }
415→
416→        Console.WriteLine($"Full save: {bytes.Length} bytes");
417→    }
418→    else
419→    {
420→        var bytes = doc.SaveIncremental();
421→        File.AppendAllBytes(incrementalPath, bytes);
422→
423→        Console.WriteLine($"Incremental save: {bytes.Length} bytes");
424→    }
425→}
426→```
427→
428→This automatically compacts when incremental updates grow too large.
429→
430→# Database Storage
431→
432→Store documents in databases:
433→
434→```csharp
435→// Save to database
436→var bytes = doc.Save();
437→await database.ExecuteAsync(
438→    "INSERT INTO documents (id, data, updated_at) VALUES (@id, @data, @updated_at)",
439→    new { id = "doc-001", data = bytes, updated_at = DateTime.UtcNow });
440→
441→// Load from database
442→var row = await database.QuerySingleAsync(
443→    "SELECT data FROM documents WHERE id = @id",
444→    new { id = "doc-001" });
445→
446→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Load(row.data);
447→```
448→
449→Binary data stores efficiently in BLOB columns (PostgreSQL bytea, SQL Server varbinary, etc.).
450→
451→# Cloud Storage
452→
453→Store documents in cloud storage services:
454→
455→```csharp
456→// Save to cloud storage (pseudo-code)
457→var bytes = doc.Save();
458→await cloudStorage.UploadAsync($"documents/{id}.automerge", bytes);
459→
460→// Load from cloud storage
461→var bytes = await cloudStorage.DownloadAsync($"documents/{id}.automerge");
462→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Load(bytes);
463→```
464→
465→Works with S3, Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage, and others.
466→
467→# Checking Pending Operations
468→
469→Check if there are uncommitted changes before saving:
470→
471→```csharp
472→var pendingOps = doc.PendingOperations;
473→
474→if (pendingOps > 0)
475→{
476→    Console.WriteLine($"Warning: {pendingOps} pending operation(s)");
477→    Console.WriteLine("Commit changes before saving");
478→
479→    doc.Commit("Auto-commit before save");
480→}
481→
482→var bytes = doc.Save();
483→```
484→
485→Uncommitted operations don't appear in saves until you commit them.
486→
487→# Versioning Documents
488→
489→Use heads to track versions:
490→
491→```csharp
492→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Create();
493→var root = doc.RootId;
494→
495→// Version 1
496→doc.PutString(root, "content", "Version 1");
497→doc.Commit("v1");
498→var v1Heads = doc.GetHeads();
499→
500→// Version 2
501→doc.PutString(root, "content", "Version 2");
502→doc.Commit("v2");
503→var v2Heads = doc.GetHeads();
504→
505→// Get changes from v1 to v2
506→var changes = doc.GetChanges(v1Heads);
507→Console.WriteLine($"Changes from v1 to v2: {changes.Count}");
508→
509→// Store version metadata
510→var versions = new Dictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<string>>
511→{
512→    ["v1"] = v1Heads,
513→    ["v2"] = v2Heads
514→};
515→```
516→
517→This enables version tracking and diffing between versions.
518→
519→# Best Practices
520→
521→## Save After Commits
522→
523→Always save after committing changes:
524→
525→```csharp
526→// Good: Save after commit
527→doc.PutString(root, "field", "value");
528→doc.Commit("Update field");
529→var bytes = doc.Save();
530→
531→// Risky: Save before commit
532→doc.PutString(root, "field", "value");
533→var bytes = doc.Save(); // Pending changes not included
534→doc.Commit("Update field");
535→```
536→
537→Uncommitted changes don't persist.
538→
539→## Use Incremental Saves for Frequent Updates
540→
541→For applications with frequent changes, use incremental saves:
542→
543→```csharp
544→// Good for frequent updates
545→doc.PutString(root, "field", "value1");
546→doc.Commit("Change 1");
547→var inc1 = doc.SaveIncremental();
548→
549→doc.PutString(root, "field", "value2");
550→doc.Commit("Change 2");
551→var inc2 = doc.SaveIncremental();
552→
553→// Periodically do full save for compaction
554→```
555→
556→## Implement Backup Strategy
557→
558→Regular backups protect against data loss:
559→
560→```csharp
561→void BackupDocument(string id, AutomergeDocument doc)
562→{
563→    var timestamp = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.ToString("yyyyMMdd-HHmmss");
564→    var backupPath = $"backups/{id}-{timestamp}.automerge";
565→
566→    var bytes = doc.Save();
567→    File.WriteAllBytes(backupPath, bytes);
568→
569→    Console.WriteLine($"Backup created: {backupPath}");
570→}
571→```
572→
573→## Validate Loaded Documents
574→
575→Check loaded documents for expected structure:
576→
577→```csharp
578→using var doc = AutomergeDocument.Load(bytes);
579→var json = doc.HydrateJson();
580→using var parsed = JsonDocument.Parse(json);
581→
582→if (!parsed.RootElement.TryGetProperty("id", out var id))
583→{
584→    throw new Exception("Invalid document: missing id field");
585→}
586→
587→Console.WriteLine($"Loaded document: {id.GetString()}");
588→```
589→
590→## Handle Load Failures
591→
592→Always handle potential load failures:
593→
594→```csharp
595→AutomergeDocument LoadDocumentSafe(string id)
596→{
597→    try
598→    {
599→        var bytes = File.ReadAllBytes($"{id}.automerge");
600→        return AutomergeDocument.Load(bytes);
601→    }
602→    catch (AutomergeException)
603→    {
604→        Console.WriteLine($"Corrupted document {id}, creating new one");
605→        return AutomergeDocument.Create();
606→    }
607→}
608→```
